---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ChapterNav from '../components/ChapterNav.astro';
import { chapterMarker, chapterNumber } from '../lib/tabs';
/* R270 [S12]: the eyebrow used to type this page's chapter number by hand,
   in a format ChapterNav did not use, next to a nav computing the same number
   from the registry. The number now comes from TABS and the format from
   chapterMarker, so reordering the registry moves both. chapterNumber throws
   here rather than rendering nothing, because a chapter whose page is not in
   the registry is a navigation dead end this page cannot detect. */
/* R217 [§S5]: the distributional section quoted 13,200 households for the top
   0.01% - the WEALTH_DIST figure, which drives the INEQUALITY chart - while
   describing the chart that runs on GROUPS.t10000, 12,000. A 10% divergence
   inside one module, published against the wrong series. Both counts are
   right about their own series; the prose was pointing at the other one.
   Rendered from the series it describes, so it cannot drift again. */
import { GROUPS, WEALTH_DIST } from '../lib/taxparams';

const topBand = GROUPS.filter((g) => g.id === 't10000')[0];
const wealthBand = WEALTH_DIST.groups.filter((g) => g.id === 'top001')[0];
const DIST_TOP_HH = Math.round(topBand.hhM * 1e6).toLocaleString('en-US');
const WEALTH_TOP_HH = Math.round(wealthBand.hhM * 1e6).toLocaleString('en-US');
---
<BaseLayout title="National Health Assurance: Taxes & Financing">
  <main>

  <section class="card chapter-intro">
    <span class="chapter-intro-eyebrow">{chapterMarker(chapterNumber('tax')!)} · Paying for it</span>
    <h2>The same care, financed differently</h2>
    <p class="desc">The healthcare chapter showed the system costs about
      what the country already spends. This chapter answers the harder
      question: who writes the checks. It starts with where the money
      actually is, builds tax packages that meet the funding goal without
      falling on lower incomes, and shows, bracket by bracket, how new
      taxes compare with the premiums and bills that disappear. The next
      chapter turns to the law that has to authorize all of it.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- The inequality context -->
  <section class="card">
    <h2>Start with where the money is</h2>
    <p class="desc">Any honest financing conversation starts here. American
      wealth has concentrated to a degree the words “top 1%” no longer
      describe; the action is inside the top tenth and hundredth of a
      percent. That is why the model on this page breaks the top 1% into
      five bands, grows their capital faster than the economy (because it
      does grow faster), and includes instruments aimed specifically at
      them.</p>
    <div class="tiles" id="inequality-tiles"></div>
    <div id="wealth-chart"></div>
    <p class="note">The wealth bands on this chart count {WEALTH_TOP_HH}
      households in the top 0.01%, against {DIST_TOP_HH} in the burden charts
      further down: the two run on different sources, Federal Reserve wealth
      bands here and CBO income bands there, and neither is a restatement of
      the other. Sources: Federal Reserve Distributional Financial
      Accounts (2026:Q1, $174.0T total household wealth) for the published
      bands; the splits inside the top 1% are derived from Saez–Zucman
      capitalization estimates and Forbes-list aggregates, constrained to
      the Fed's band totals (medium confidence). Median household net worth
      from the Survey of Consumer Finances.</p>

    <h2 style="font-size:15px;margin-top:24px">Why the ultra-wealthy are hard to tax</h2>
    <ul class="lever-list">
      <li><b>Income is optional at the top.</b> The tax code mostly taxes
        realized income, and a billionaire can simply not realize any:
        hold appreciating assets, borrow against them for spending, and
        let the step-up at death erase the gain entirely. “Buy, borrow,
        die” is why measured top-0.01% tax rates fall below the merely
        rich.</li>
      <li><b>Valuation and liquidity.</b> Private businesses, art, and
        carried interests are hard to appraise annually, and taxing them
        can force sales; every wealth-tax design fights this.</li>
      <li><b>A single instrument is easy to unwind.</b> Grantor trusts,
        dynasty trusts, valuation discounts, and pass-through games all exist
        to move wealth around the estate tax, and the estate tax now collects
        a fraction of what it once would: run the 2000 tax code against 2019
        estates and it would have raised about nine times the liability it
        actually did. But the Penn Wharton Budget Model's decomposition of
        that gap puts most of it on statutory changes, the raised exemption
        and cut rates, rather than on the planning industry. That is the
        harder lesson for a financing plan. Avoidance is real and it is the
        smaller half; the larger half is that one instrument, standing alone,
        can be legislated away.
        <a href="https://budgetmodel.wharton.upenn.edu/p/2022-07-28-decomposing-the-decline-in-estate-tax-liability-since-2000/"
          target="_blank" rel="noopener">Penn Wharton Budget Model, 2022</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Legal uncertainty.</b> A federal wealth tax faces the
        Constitution's direct-tax apportionment clause, and taxing
        unrealized gains tests the 16th Amendment's meaning of income
        (Moore v. United States left the question open, not answered).</li>
      <li><b>Enforcement asymmetry.</b> Audit rates fell across every income
        level from tax year 2010 to 2019, from 0.9% to 0.25% overall, and fell
        furthest for returns reporting $200,000 or more. The GAO attributes it
        to staffing: a complex high-income audit costs more examiner time than
        the agency had. The taxpayers with the most to gain from opacity were
        the ones least likely to be looked at.
        <a href="https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-22-104960"
          target="_blank" rel="noopener">GAO-22-104960</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2 style="font-size:15px;margin-top:18px">How it can realistically be done</h2>
    <ul class="lever-list">
      <li><b>Tax what cannot flee or hide.</b> Withholding and third-party
        reporting on capital income, an uncapped payroll base, and
        transaction taxes collect at the source, before planning starts.</li>
      <li><b>Close the death loophole first.</b> Ending stepped-up basis
        and taxing gains at death removes the exit that makes deferral a
        permanent escape; every rate increase works better once it is
        shut.</li>
      <li><b>Minimum taxes on comprehensive income.</b> The billionaire
        minimum tax sidesteps game-by-game whack-a-mole by asking one
        question: did you pay at least 25% on your true economic gain?</li>
      <li><b>Tax inheritances as income to heirs.</b> Constitutionally
        clean (it is income), progressive by construction, and aimed at
        dynasties rather than founders.</li>
      <li><b>Fund the referees.</b> High-end enforcement collects tax
        already owed; the return on IRS funding at the top is documented
        and large.</li>
      <li><b>Coordinate internationally and at the exit.</b> Pillar-2
        minimum taxes, information exchange, and a real exit tax shrink
        the run-away option.</li>
      <li><b>Build a legal ladder, not a single rung.</b> The plan's
        own contingency matrix: if the wealth tax falls in court, fall back
        to mark-to-market; then to realization-at-death plus minimum
        taxes. Every scenario below survives losing its most contested
        instrument.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="font-size:15px;margin-top:24px">It has been done before</h2>
    <p class="desc">America taxed its top earners far harder than it does
      now, for decades, through its strongest growth years: the top
      marginal rate stayed at 91 to 92 percent through the entire
      Eisenhower administration and did not fall below 70 percent until
      1982. It is 37 percent today.</p>
    <div id="toprate-chart"></div>
    <p class="note">Source: IRS Statistics of Income historical tables.
      Effective rates paid were lower than statutory rates in every era
      (deductions, exclusions, capital gains); the line shows what the law
      asked of the next dollar at the very top, which is the policy choice
      Congress controls.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Funding programs -->
  <section class="card">
    <h2>What needs funding</h2>
    <p class="desc">The tax model is general: it funds <em>programs</em>, each
      with its own need over time. National Health Assurance's new-revenue
      requirement flows in live from the healthcare model (change assumptions
      or scenarios there and the need updates here). Add other programs, such as
      childcare, climate, or deficit reduction, to see combined financing
      demands.</p>
    <div class="program-list" id="program-list"></div>
    <div class="program-add">
      <input class="p-name" id="p-add-name" placeholder="Program name" aria-label="Program name">
      <input class="p-amt" id="p-add-amt" type="number" placeholder="$B/yr" aria-label="Annual amount in billions">
      <input class="p-start" id="p-add-start" type="number" value="2030" min="2027" max="2042" aria-label="Start year">
      <input class="p-ramp" id="p-add-ramp" type="number" value="3" min="0" max="10" aria-label="Ramp years">
      <button id="p-add-btn">Add program</button>
      <span class="note" style="margin:0">amount ($B/yr at maturity) · start year · ramp-in years</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Instruments -->
  <section class="card">
    <h2>The tax package: pick a scenario that meets the goal, then make it yours</h2>
    <p class="desc">Sixteen instruments spanning the realistic menu, from
      progressive surtaxes and uncapped payroll to the wealth tax,
      billionaire minimum, and inheritance-as-income. Each carries a sourced
      revenue estimate, its own base-growth rate (top-capital bases compound
      at 4% real, wages at 1.2%, broad income at 1.9%), a start year, and a
      slider or toggle. The scenarios below are solved to actually cover the
      funding need; select one, then edit anything.</p>
    <div class="control" style="max-width:520px">
      <label for="tax-scenario">Financing scenario</label>
      <select id="tax-scenario" aria-label="Financing scenario"></select>
    </div>
    <p class="scenario-desc" id="tax-scenario-desc"></p>
    <div class="tax-controls" id="tax-instruments"></div>
    <p class="note" id="tax-overlap-note"></p>
    <p class="note">None of the goal scenarios uses the consumption tax:
      a VAT is regressive (the bottom quintile consumes about twice its
      income), so it stays out of every preset. It remains available above
      for anyone who wants to see exactly what that trade-off does to the
      burden chart below.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Revenue vs need over time -->
  <section class="card">
    <h2>Revenue vs. need, 2027–2042</h2>
    <p class="desc">Each instrument phases in on its own schedule; bases grow
      with the economy. The dark line is the total funding need of every
      enabled program; the stack has to reach it.</p>
    <div id="tax-path-chart"></div>
    <div class="tiles" id="tax-tiles"></div>
  </section>

  <!-- Save vs pay: the honest ledger per bracket -->
  <section class="card">
    <h2>Yes, taxes rise for every bracket. Here is the other side of the ledger.</h2>
    <p class="desc">Every goal scenario raises taxes across the income
      scale; there is no way to fund a $3T+ program otherwise. But taxes
      are only half of each household's ledger: the same reform deletes
      premiums, deductibles, copays, and surprise bills, and passes
      employer premium savings back through wages. This chart puts both
      halves side by side for every bracket in
      <span id="savepay-year">2041</span>, under whatever scenario and
      edits are selected above. Bars are drawn as a share of income so an
      $8,000 saving for a working family and an $8M tax bill for a
      billionaire can share one picture; hover for exact dollars.</p>
    <div id="tax-savepay-chart"></div>
    <p class="note">Savings distribute the healthcare model's replaced
      household spending (BLS CES pattern) plus wage pass-through by wage
      share; taxes are the selected package's incidence. The net column
      matches the burden chart below, which shows the same result as a
      single bar per group. Roughly the bottom 90% comes out ahead in
      dollars; the crossover sits near the 90th to 99th percentile
      depending on the scenario.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Distributional impact -->
  <section class="card">
    <h2>Who bears the burden, and who comes out ahead</h2>
    <p class="desc">The question that matters for lower-income Americans:
      new taxes <em>minus</em> the health costs this system eliminates
      (premiums, deductibles, copays), by income group. The top 1% is
      broken into five bands, down to the {DIST_TOP_HH} households of the top
      0.01%, because that is where the wealth actually sits; the
      millionaire surtax, billionaire minimum tax, wealth tax, estate
      restoration, inheritance tax, and capital-gains reform in the package
      above land almost entirely on those bands, which is also why their
      revenues are not added together: they reach one base, so the model
      nets them and reports the deduction on the revenue tiles above.
      Bars left of zero
      mean a group's total annual costs go <em>down</em>. View in
      <label style="display:inline">
        year <select id="dist-year" aria-label="Distribution year"></select>
      </label>
      <span class="mode-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Units">
        <button id="mode-dollars" class="active">$ per household</button>
        <button id="mode-pct">% of income</button>
      </span>
    </p>
    <div id="tax-impact-chart"></div>
    <h2 style="font-size:14.5px;margin-top:20px">Average federal tax rate, current law → with this package</h2>
    <p class="desc">Pure tax-side view (health savings excluded), comparable
      to CBO's effective-rate tables.</p>
    <div id="tax-rate-chart"></div>
    <details class="tableview"><summary>View distribution as table</summary>
      <div class="tbl-scroll" id="tax-dist-table"></div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <!-- Tax methodology -->
  <section class="card">
    <h2>Tax model methodology</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <b>Structure:</b> income groups follow CBO's distribution format
      (quintiles, 80–90th, 90–99th, top 1%). Each instrument carries a sourced
      revenue estimate at 2024 scale (growing with the economy), a phase-in
      schedule, and an incidence vector (who ultimately bears it), using
      CBO/JCT conventions (employer payroll → workers' wages; corporate →
      25% labor / 75% capital; consumption taxes → consumption shares).
    </div>
    <div class="note">
      <b>Sources:</b> the income-group table is CBO's <em>Distribution of
      Household Income, 2022</em> (published January 2026), extracted from
      CBO's own supplemental workbook and inflated to 2024 dollars; revenue
      scores come from CBO's <em>Options for Reducing the Deficit:
      2025–2034</em> (December 2024, JCT staff), the Treasury FY2025
      Greenbook, and the Saez–Zucman wealth-tax letter; health spending by
      quintile is BLS Consumer Expenditure Survey 2024 (healthcare runs
      20.7% of income in the lowest quintile vs 3.7% in the highest; that is the
      regressivity this system unwinds). Full citations in
      <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System/blob/main/research/06_tax_distribution_financing.md"
         target="_blank" rel="noopener">research file 06</a>.
    </div>
    <div class="note">
      <b>Limits:</b> incidence is static (no behavioral responses
      beyond the haircuts already in each estimate); group boundaries don't
      capture within-group variation (a "middle quintile" family with high
      health costs gains far more than one without); the wealth tax has no
      official JCT/CBO score and its legal survival is genuinely uncertain
      (the plan carries a fallback matrix for that reason); the estate
      instrument has no current score against the post-2025 $15M exemption;
      and CBO's December 2024 option scores predate the July 2025
      reconciliation act, so some would re-base lower today.
    </div>
  </section>

    <ChapterNav />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../scripts/tax-client.ts';
</script>
